1
00:01:34,999 --> 00:01:36,919
Time to get up, man.

2
00:01:38,759 --> 00:01:41,039
-All right, Dad.
-Come on.

3
00:02:48,879 --> 00:02:50,759
Should be here soon.

4
00:03:38,239 --> 00:03:40,399
-I think I should make a list.
-What do mean?

5
00:03:40,599 --> 00:03:42,999
-For your birthday gifts?
-Yeah.

6
00:03:43,199 --> 00:03:45,719
You know you're only getting
a couple of things, right?

7
00:03:45,959 --> 00:03:49,999
Yeah, I know. Just to look at
and study so I can choose better.

8
00:03:50,239 --> 00:03:53,159
Okay, well, that's smart.
Yeah, make a list.

9
00:03:53,639 --> 00:03:55,559
Can you spell everything
you're thinking of?

10
00:03:55,759 --> 00:03:59,359
-I think so.
-All right. That's good.

11
00:04:03,319 --> 00:04:06,199
-How you doing in here, man?
-Okay.

12
00:04:13,439 --> 00:04:16,399
Can we go to the park today, after?

13
00:04:17,199 --> 00:04:20,679
No, I gotta go to Oakland.
Well, maybe, we'll see.

14
00:04:20,879 --> 00:04:22,439
Give me a kiss.

15
00:04:23,319 --> 00:04:25,239
I'll talk to you later.

16
00:04:28,639 --> 00:04:30,319
Excuse me.

17
00:04:31,079 --> 00:04:32,839
Oh, excuse me...

18
00:04:33,039 --> 00:04:35,279
...when is somebody
gonna clean this off?

19
00:04:38,319 --> 00:04:40,879
And the Y?
The Y. We talked about this.

20
00:04:41,079 --> 00:04:44,479
It's an I in "happiness."
There's no Y in "happiness." It's an l.

21
00:04:46,759 --> 00:04:48,639
I'm Chris Gardner.

22
00:04:49,079 --> 00:04:52,159
I met my father for the first time
when I was 28 years old.

23
00:04:52,959 --> 00:04:54,599
And I made up my mind
as a young kid...

24
00:04:54,799 --> 00:04:56,359
...that when I had children...

25
00:04:56,559 --> 00:04:59,879
...my children were gonna know
who their father was.

26
00:05:01,759 --> 00:05:04,519
This is part of my life story.

27
00:05:05,279 --> 00:05:08,679
This part is called "Riding the Bus. "

28
00:05:15,159 --> 00:05:16,839
What's that?

29
00:05:17,439 --> 00:05:19,679
It's a time machine, isn't it?

30
00:05:20,479 --> 00:05:22,079
Seems like a time machine.

31
00:05:22,279 --> 00:05:25,359
That seems like a time machine.
It's a time machine. Take me with you.

32
00:05:25,599 --> 00:05:26,759
This machine...

33
00:05:27,239 --> 00:05:28,479
...this machine on my lap--

34
00:05:28,679 --> 00:05:30,639
This guy, he has a time machine.

35
00:05:30,839 --> 00:05:33,919
He travels in the past
with this machine and....

36
00:05:34,119 --> 00:05:36,359
--it is not a time machine.

37
00:05:36,919 --> 00:05:39,519
It's a portable
bone-density scanner.

38
00:05:40,359 --> 00:05:42,999
A medical device I sell for a living.

39
00:05:43,199 --> 00:05:45,679
Thank you for the opportunity
to discuss it with you.

40
00:05:45,919 --> 00:05:47,999
-I appreciate it.
-We just don't need it, Chris.

41
00:05:48,199 --> 00:05:49,959
It's unnecessary and expensive.

42
00:05:50,159 --> 00:05:52,319
-Well, maybe next--
-Thank you.

43
00:05:53,359 --> 00:05:58,479
It gave a slightly denser picture
than an x-ray for twice the money.

44
00:06:01,479 --> 00:06:03,079
-Hey.
-Hey, baby.

45
00:06:03,279 --> 00:06:04,959
-What happened?
-No, nothing.

46
00:06:05,159 --> 00:06:06,719
Look, I can't get Christopher today.

47
00:06:07,239 --> 00:06:09,439
Oh, no, you don't, Chris.
I'm back on at 7.

48
00:06:09,679 --> 00:06:11,399
I know. I have got to go to Oakland.

49
00:06:11,879 --> 00:06:14,759
So I gotta get Christopher home,
feed him, bathe him...

50
00:06:14,959 --> 00:06:17,559
...get him in bed,
and be back here by 7?

51
00:06:17,759 --> 00:06:19,879
-Yes.
-And we got the tax-bill notice today.

52
00:06:20,119 --> 00:06:23,639
-What are you gonna do about that?
-Look, this is what we gotta do.

53
00:06:27,959 --> 00:06:32,159
You see that car? The one
with the pretty yellow shoe on it?

54
00:06:32,359 --> 00:06:33,719
That's mine.

55
00:06:33,919 --> 00:06:35,879
There's no parking near hospitals.

56
00:06:36,079 --> 00:06:38,559
That's what happens
when you're always in a rush.

57
00:06:38,799 --> 00:06:40,679
Thanks anyway. Very much.

58
00:06:40,879 --> 00:06:43,839
-Maybe next quarter.
-It's possible.

59
00:06:44,679 --> 00:06:50,239
I needed to sell at least two scanners
a month for rent and daycare.

60
00:06:51,119 --> 00:06:52,479
I'd have to sell one more...

61
00:06:52,679 --> 00:06:56,439
...to pay off all of those tickets
under my windshield wiper.

62
00:07:03,239 --> 00:07:05,239
The problem is...

63
00:07:06,159 --> 00:07:08,639
...I haven't sold any for a while.

64
00:07:10,319 --> 00:07:12,999
Since when do you not like
macaroni and cheese?

65
00:07:13,239 --> 00:07:15,759
Since birth?

66
00:07:18,239 --> 00:07:20,239
-What's that?
-What?

67
00:07:20,479 --> 00:07:23,559
-What is this?
-It's a gift for Christopher.

68
00:07:23,759 --> 00:07:26,239
-From who?
-Cynthia from work.

69
00:07:26,479 --> 00:07:29,559
It's for adults. Chris can't use it.
She didn't know.

70
00:07:29,759 --> 00:07:31,759
What are you supposed
to do with it?

71
00:07:32,479 --> 00:07:35,559
Make every side the same color.

72
00:07:36,839 --> 00:07:39,159
Did you pay the taxes?

73
00:07:39,439 --> 00:07:42,559
No, I'm gonna
have to file an extension.

74
00:07:43,359 --> 00:07:47,519
-You already filed an extension.
-Yeah, well, I gotta file another one.

75
00:07:47,879 --> 00:07:51,599
That's-- It's $650.
I'll have it in the next month.

76
00:07:52,079 --> 00:07:53,919
That means interest, right?

77
00:07:54,119 --> 00:07:56,479
-And a penalty?
-Yeah, a little bit.

78
00:07:56,719 --> 00:08:02,319
Look, why don't you let me do this?
All right, just relax. Okay?

79
00:08:02,519 --> 00:08:06,719
-Come here. Calm down.
-I have to go back to work.

80
00:08:14,719 --> 00:08:18,359
Let's get ready for bed.
Hey, put your plate in the sink.

81
00:08:19,039 --> 00:08:21,999
A few days ago I was presented
with a report I'd asked for...

82
00:08:22,199 --> 00:08:25,919
...a comprehensive audit, if you will,
of our economic condition.

83
00:08:26,599 --> 00:08:29,319
You won't like it. I didn't like it.

84
00:08:29,519 --> 00:08:31,199
But we have to face the truth...

85
00:08:31,399 --> 00:08:34,039
...and then go to work
to turn things around.

86
00:08:34,239 --> 00:08:37,999
And make no mistake about it,
we can turn them around.

87
00:08:38,199 --> 00:08:41,319
The federal budget is out of control.

88
00:08:41,519 --> 00:08:44,879
And we face runaway deficits
of almost $80 billion...

89
00:08:45,079 --> 00:08:48,519
...for this budget year
that ends September 30th.

90
00:08:48,719 --> 00:08:53,959
That deficit is larger than
the entire federal budget in 1 957.

91
00:08:54,159 --> 00:08:56,719
And so is the almost $80 billion...

92
00:08:56,919 --> 00:09:01,239
... we will pay in interest
this year on the national debt.

93
00:09:01,879 --> 00:09:04,399
Twenty years ago, in 1 960...

94
00:09:04,599 --> 00:09:08,759
...our federal government payroll
was less than $ 1 3 billion.

95
00:09:08,959 --> 00:09:11,679
Today it is 75 billion.

96
00:09:11,879 --> 00:09:17,439
During these 20 years, our population
has only increased by 23. 3 percent....

97
00:09:35,599 --> 00:09:38,359
Man, I got two questions for you:

98
00:09:38,559 --> 00:09:41,679
What do you do?
And how do you do it?

99
00:09:43,199 --> 00:09:46,319
-I'm a stockbroker.
-Stockbroker. Oh, goodness.

100
00:09:46,519 --> 00:09:48,479
Had to go to college
to be a stockbroker, huh?

101
00:09:48,759 --> 00:09:52,439
You don't have to. Have to be good
with numbers and good with people.

102
00:09:52,639 --> 00:09:55,039
-That's it.
-Hey, you take care.

103
00:09:55,239 --> 00:09:57,319
I'll let you hang on to my car
for the weekend.

104
00:09:57,519 --> 00:09:59,999
-But I need it back for Monday.
-Feed the meter.

105
00:10:07,159 --> 00:10:09,559
I still remember that moment.

106
00:10:13,839 --> 00:10:18,519
They all looked
so damn happy to me.

107
00:10:25,679 --> 00:10:28,079
Why couldn't I look like that?

108
00:10:45,679 --> 00:10:47,039
I'm gonna try to get home by 6.

109
00:10:47,239 --> 00:10:50,359
I'm gonna stop by a brokerage firm
after work.

110
00:10:50,559 --> 00:10:53,839
-For what?
-I wanna see about a job there.

111
00:10:54,039 --> 00:10:56,679
Yeah? What job?

112
00:10:57,199 --> 00:10:59,199
You know, when l--

113
00:10:59,399 --> 00:11:02,039
When I was a kid, I could go through
a math book in a week.

114
00:11:02,239 --> 00:11:05,599
So I'm gonna go see about
what job they got down there.

115
00:11:06,679 --> 00:11:08,639
What job?

116
00:11:11,599 --> 00:11:13,319
Stockbroker.

117
00:11:15,639 --> 00:11:18,839
-Stockbroker?
-Yeah.

118
00:11:19,239 --> 00:11:21,239
Not an astronaut?

119
00:11:38,239 --> 00:11:39,719
Don't talk to me like that, Linda.

120
00:11:40,839 --> 00:11:44,639
I'm gonna go down and see about this,
and I'm gonna do it during the day.

121
00:11:44,879 --> 00:11:46,559
You should probably
do your sales calls.

122
00:11:47,039 --> 00:11:49,479
I don't need you to tell me
about my sales calls, Linda.

123
00:11:49,679 --> 00:11:52,879
I got three of them
before the damn office is even open.

124
00:11:53,399 --> 00:11:55,839
Do you remember
that rent is due next week?

125
00:11:56,039 --> 00:11:57,919
Probably not.

126
00:11:58,559 --> 00:12:00,599
We're already two months behind.

127
00:12:00,799 --> 00:12:03,839
Next week we'll owe three months.

128
00:12:04,319 --> 00:12:08,439
I've been pulling double shifts
for four months now, Chris.

129
00:12:08,839 --> 00:12:13,159
Just sell what's in your contract.
Get us out of that business.

130
00:12:13,359 --> 00:12:17,039
Linda, that is what I am trying to do.

131
00:12:17,799 --> 00:12:20,479
This is what I'm trying to do
for my family...

132
00:12:20,679 --> 00:12:22,079
...for you and for Christopher.

133
00:12:23,519 --> 00:12:25,599
What's the matter with you?

134
00:12:29,759 --> 00:12:31,479
Linda.

135
00:12:32,039 --> 00:12:33,559
Linda.

136
00:13:00,559 --> 00:13:05,679
This part of my life
is called "Being Stupid. "

137
00:13:07,719 --> 00:13:08,999
Can I ask you a favor, miss?

138
00:13:09,199 --> 00:13:13,079
Do you mind if I leave this here
with you just for five minutes?

139
00:13:13,279 --> 00:13:15,759
I have a meeting in there
and I don't wanna carry that...

140
00:13:15,999 --> 00:13:17,999
...Iooking smalltime.

141
00:13:18,839 --> 00:13:23,279
Here is a dollar and I'll give you
more money when I come back out.

142
00:13:23,479 --> 00:13:26,759
Okay? It's not valuable.
You can't sell it anywhere.

143
00:13:27,679 --> 00:13:31,879
I can't even sell it, and it's my job.
All right?

144
00:13:39,559 --> 00:13:41,919
-Chris? Tim Brophy, Resources.
-Yes. How are you?

145
00:13:42,119 --> 00:13:43,439
-Come with me.
-Yes, sir.

146
00:13:43,639 --> 00:13:46,399
Let me see if I can find you
an application for our internship.

147
00:13:46,599 --> 00:13:49,839
I'm afraid that's all we can do for you.
See, this is a satellite office.

148
00:13:50,039 --> 00:13:52,719
Jay Twistle in the main office,
he oversees Witter Resources.

149
00:13:52,919 --> 00:13:54,999
I mean, I'm-- You know,
I'm just this office.

150
00:13:55,239 --> 00:13:58,159
As you can see, we got a hell of lot
of applications here, so....

151
00:13:58,359 --> 00:14:01,399
Normally I have a resume sheet,
but I can't seem to find it anywhere.

152
00:14:01,599 --> 00:14:04,999
-We....
-Thank you very much.

153
00:14:05,199 --> 00:14:06,359
I need to go.

154
00:14:06,959 --> 00:14:08,679
I'll bring this back.

155
00:14:08,919 --> 00:14:12,039
-Thank you.
-Okay.

156
00:14:18,399 --> 00:14:22,919
Trusting a hippie girl with my scanner.
Why did I do that?

157
00:14:23,119 --> 00:14:24,759
Excuse me. Excuse me.

158
00:14:24,959 --> 00:14:30,399
Like I said, this part of my life
is called "Being Stupid. "

159
00:14:33,559 --> 00:14:38,279
Hey!. Hey!. Hey!. Don't move!.
Don't move!. Stay--!.

160
00:14:46,239 --> 00:14:48,599
Stop!. Stop!.

161
00:14:48,799 --> 00:14:51,999
Don't move!.
Stop this--!. Stop the train!

162
00:14:53,039 --> 00:14:55,359
Stop! Stop!

163
00:15:10,279 --> 00:15:13,999
The program took just 20 people
every six months.

164
00:15:14,199 --> 00:15:16,279
One got the job.

165
00:15:17,839 --> 00:15:23,199
There were three blank lines after
"high school" to list more education.

166
00:15:24,759 --> 00:15:27,559
I didn't need that many lines.

167
00:15:32,639 --> 00:15:35,599
T ry and sleep. It's late.

168
00:15:45,599 --> 00:15:49,199
It's a puzzle measuring just 3 inches
by 3 inches on each side...

169
00:15:49,399 --> 00:15:51,839
...made up of multiple colors
that you twist and turn...

170
00:15:52,039 --> 00:15:54,639
...and try to get
to a solid color on each side.

171
00:15:54,839 --> 00:15:58,479
This little cube
is the gift sensation of 1 98 1.

172
00:15:58,679 --> 00:16:00,079
Don't expect to solve it easily.

173
00:16:00,279 --> 00:16:02,719
Although we did encounter
one math professor at USF...

174
00:16:02,919 --> 00:16:05,399
... who took just 30 minutes on his.

175
00:16:05,639 --> 00:16:07,839
This is as far as I've gotten
on mine.

176
00:16:08,039 --> 00:16:11,439
As you can see,
I still have a long way to go.

177
00:16:11,639 --> 00:16:14,239
This is Jim Finnerty reporting
for KJSF in Richmond.

178
00:16:14,479 --> 00:16:17,159
Hey, wake up.

179
00:16:17,359 --> 00:16:18,879
Eat.

180
00:16:19,999 --> 00:16:22,479
-Bye, Mom.
-Bye, baby.

181
00:16:22,679 --> 00:16:25,679
-Come back without that, please.
-Oh, yeah, I'm going to.

182
00:16:25,879 --> 00:16:28,799
So go ahead, say goodbye to it,
because I'm coming back without it.

183
00:16:28,999 --> 00:16:31,439
Goodbye and good riddance.

184
00:16:31,639 --> 00:16:33,599
You ain't had to add
the "good riddance" part.

185
00:16:33,799 --> 00:16:34,879
Bye, Mom.

186
00:16:36,399 --> 00:16:37,759
Bye.

187
00:16:37,959 --> 00:16:41,559
It's written as P-P-Y, but it's
supposed to be an I in "happiness."

188
00:16:41,759 --> 00:16:44,879
-ls it an adjective?
-No, actually it's a noun.

189
00:16:45,079 --> 00:16:46,839
But it's not spelled right.

190
00:16:47,039 --> 00:16:50,639
-ls "fuck" spelled right?
-Yeah, that's spelled right.

191
00:16:50,839 --> 00:16:53,959
But that's not part of the motto,
so you're not supposed to learn that.

192
00:16:54,159 --> 00:16:59,079
That's an adult word to show anger
and other things.

193
00:16:59,279 --> 00:17:02,159
-But just don't use that one, okay?
-Okay.

194
00:17:02,359 --> 00:17:04,519
What's that say
on the back of your bag?

195
00:17:04,839 --> 00:17:06,799
My nickname.

196
00:17:06,999 --> 00:17:08,599
We pick nicknames.

197
00:17:08,799 --> 00:17:12,199
-Oh, yeah? What's it say?
-"Hot Rod."

198
00:17:15,199 --> 00:17:17,199
-Did you have a nickname?
-Yep.

199
00:17:17,439 --> 00:17:20,159
-What?
-"Ten-Gallon Head."

200
00:17:20,359 --> 00:17:23,279
-What's that?
-I grew up in Louisiana, near Texas.

201
00:17:23,479 --> 00:17:28,079
Everybody wears cowboy hats.
And a ten-gallon's a big hat.

202
00:17:28,319 --> 00:17:31,879
I was smart back then,
so they called me Ten-Gallon Head.

203
00:17:32,479 --> 00:17:34,599
-Hoss wears that hat.
-Hoss?

204
00:17:34,879 --> 00:17:36,599
Hoss Cartwright on Bonanza.

205
00:17:37,039 --> 00:17:40,559
-How do you know Bonanza?
-We watch it at Mrs. Chu's.

206
00:17:41,239 --> 00:17:44,359
-You watch Bonanza at daycare?
-Yeah.

207
00:17:45,439 --> 00:17:47,479
When? When do you watch it?

208
00:17:47,679 --> 00:17:51,359
-After snack? After your nap?
-After Love Boat.

209
00:17:56,279 --> 00:17:58,439
I made my list for my birthday.

210
00:17:59,199 --> 00:18:03,719
-Yeah, what'd you put on there?
-A basketball or an ant farm.

211
00:18:07,359 --> 00:18:11,599
-He says he's been watching TV.
-Oh, little TV for history.

212
00:18:11,799 --> 00:18:14,919
-Love Boat?
-For history. Navy.

213
00:18:15,599 --> 00:18:17,399
That's not the Navy.

214
00:18:17,599 --> 00:18:19,719
I mean, he could
watch television at home.

215
00:18:19,919 --> 00:18:24,279
We're paying you $1 50 a month.
If he's gonna be sitting around...

216
00:18:24,479 --> 00:18:26,879
...watching TV all day,
we're taking him out of here.

217
00:18:27,079 --> 00:18:30,879
Go pay more at other daycare
if you don't like Navy TV.

218
00:18:32,759 --> 00:18:37,959
You late pay anyway.
You complain. I complain.

219
00:18:39,479 --> 00:18:43,279
Can you at least put the dog upstairs
in your room or something?

220
00:18:43,479 --> 00:18:44,959
Bye.

221
00:19:29,319 --> 00:19:33,239
I was waiting for
Witter Resource head Jay Twistle...

222
00:19:33,479 --> 00:19:38,959
...whose name sounded so delightful,
like he'd give me a job and a hug.

223
00:19:39,159 --> 00:19:43,159
I just had to show him I was good
with numbers and good with people.

224
00:19:43,359 --> 00:19:44,919
-Morning, Mr. Twistle.
-Good morning.

225
00:19:45,159 --> 00:19:46,559
-Mr. Twistle, Chris Gardner.
-Hi.

226
00:19:46,759 --> 00:19:49,559
I wanted to drop this off personally
and make your acquaintance.

227
00:19:49,759 --> 00:19:53,159
I thought I'd catch you on the way in.
I'd love the opportunity to discuss...

228
00:19:53,359 --> 00:19:55,559
...what may seem like weaknesses
on my application.

229
00:19:55,799 --> 00:19:58,599
We'll start with this, and we'll call you
if we wanna sit down.

230
00:19:58,799 --> 00:20:01,759
-Yes, sir. You have a great day.
-You too.

231
00:20:08,799 --> 00:20:10,399
Hey, yeah, how you doing?

232
00:20:10,599 --> 00:20:12,799
This is Chris Gardner calling
for Dr. Delsey.

233
00:20:12,999 --> 00:20:15,159
Yeah, I'm running a little late
for a sales call.

234
00:20:15,359 --> 00:20:18,879
I was wondering if--
Yeah, Osteo National.

235
00:20:19,119 --> 00:20:21,639
Right. We can still--? Half an hour?

236
00:20:21,839 --> 00:20:25,519
Yes. Beautiful. Beautiful.
Thank you, thank you.

237
00:20:35,039 --> 00:20:38,199
Hey! Hey!

238
00:20:39,279 --> 00:20:40,679
Hey!

239
00:20:40,879 --> 00:20:42,239
This part of my life...

240
00:20:42,439 --> 00:20:45,119
-Wait!
-...this part here...

241
00:20:46,719 --> 00:20:48,319
...it's called "Running. "

242
00:20:48,519 --> 00:20:50,479
Hey! Hey!

243
00:20:50,679 --> 00:20:52,239
Wait!

244
00:20:53,159 --> 00:20:55,959
Hey! Wait!

245
00:20:56,159 --> 00:20:58,559
That was my stolen machine.

246
00:20:58,759 --> 00:21:00,799
Unless she was with
a guy who sold them too.

247
00:21:02,359 --> 00:21:03,599
Which was unlikely...

248
00:21:03,799 --> 00:21:06,439
...because I was the only one
selling them in the Bay Area.

249
00:21:06,639 --> 00:21:09,319
I spent our entire life savings
on these things.

250
00:21:11,719 --> 00:21:15,319
It was such
a revolutionary machine.

251
00:21:16,479 --> 00:21:17,879
-Can you feel it, baby?
-Oh, yeah.

252
00:21:18,119 --> 00:21:19,639
You got me doing all the work.

253
00:21:19,839 --> 00:21:22,359
What I didn't know
is that doctors and hospitals...

254
00:21:22,559 --> 00:21:26,439
...would consider them
unnecessary luxuries.

255
00:21:29,079 --> 00:21:32,519
I even asked the landlord
to take a picture.

256
00:21:33,799 --> 00:21:38,679
So if I lost one, it was like losing
a month's groceries.

257
00:21:41,599 --> 00:21:44,559
Hey, hey! Wait! Wait!

258
00:21:47,439 --> 00:21:50,119
Hey, get back here!

259
00:21:54,719 --> 00:21:57,199
Hey, man, l--

260
00:21:59,039 --> 00:22:01,359
-Who's he?
-He's that guy....

261
00:22:11,839 --> 00:22:15,759
